  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098036/assets/a3fb5a3a6102485294de519cc1372b7a348235317336a1d4c7fdbcf88be019b2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=3,$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(3)=3$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=3$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=3+8=11.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=11.$",
